Promises in JavaScript are like a contract between the code that executes first (known as the "executor") and any other code that wants to use the result of that execution later (known as the "consumer"). The promise represents the potential outcome or value that will be returned at some point in the future, either when the operation completes successfully (fulfillment) or when an error occurs (rejection).

In simpler terms, promises allow you to write asynchronous code that doesn't block the main thread, making your applications more responsive and efficient. They also provide a cleaner and more concise way to handle complex operations that may take a long time to complete, such as making HTTP requests or reading files from disk. By using promises instead of callbacks, you can avoid the pyramid-of-doom problem and make your code easier to read, understand, and test.

Here's a simple example:

```javascript
const fetchData = () => {
  return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
    setTimeout(() => {
      const data = 'This is the data';
      resolve(data); // fulfilled with the data
    }, 2000);
  });
};

fetchData()
  .then(result => console.log(result)) // prints "This is the data" after 2 seconds
  .catch(error => console.error(error)); // handles any errors during fulfillment or rejection
```

In this example, `fetchData` returns a promise that resolves after 2 seconds with a string representing the data. You can then chain additional functions (`.then()`) to process the result further, or catch any errors (`.catch()`) that occur during fulfillment or rejection. This allows you to write more modular and composable code, while still maintaining the flexibility and expressiveness of asynchronous programming.

Here's a Javascript function that takes a string variable in dot notation and returns all objects in an array where a nested property matches the given value:

```javascript
function findObjectsByPropertyValue(array, propertyPath, value) {
  // Split property path into an array of keys
  let propKeys = propertyPath.split('.');

  // Define recursive helper function to traverse object hierarchy
  const traverseObject = (obj) => {
    for (let I = 0; I < obj.length; i++) {
      if (hasMatchingProperty(obj[i], propKeys, value)) {
        yield obj[i];
      }

      // Recursively traverse nested objects
      if (typeof obj[i] === 'object') {
        yield *traverseObject(obj[i]);
      }
    }
  };

  // Helper function to check if object has all properties in propKeys
  const hasMatchingProperty = (obj, keys, val) => {
    for (let I = 0; I < keys.length; i++) {
      if (!obj.hasOwnProperty(keys[i])) {
        return false;
      }
      obj = obj[keys[i]];
    }
    return obj === val;
  };

  // Call helper function with original array and first key in propertyPath
  return [...traverseObject(array)];
}
```

